#!/usr/bin/env node

// Stamps a per-feature tippecanoe `minzoom` on terrain points (SLOGRD, LNDELV,
// SLOTOP) so a summit cluster thins with zoom NAMED AND HIGHEST FIRST, and
// derives _TERRAIN: one label anchor per surviving named summit.
//
// Usage:
//   bin/generate-terrain-minzooms <terrain.geojson> --minzoom N --maxzoom N \
//     --class SLOGRD:SLOGRD.geojson [--class LNDELV:...] [--class SLOTOP:...]
//
// Each --class file is rewritten in place. Only POINT features are touched; the
// line and area members of the classes (contour lines, slope-ground areas) keep
// whatever they had.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS
//
// Nothing between the tiles and the screen thins these points. The partition
// strips SCAMIN from the style (the producer's own decluttering), no Plotroom
// zoom floor covers the family, and both point layers draw with
// icon-allow-overlap/icon-ignore-placement, so MapLibre's collision index
// cannot help either. Measured result over Alaska: up to 63 HILTOP01/POSGEN04
// symbols inside a single 64 x 64 px square at z5. A per-band style floor
// cannot fix that -- it moves the whole pile from one zoom to the next -- so
// the decision is made here, per feature, before tippecanoe sees them.
//
// This is bin/generate-hazard-minzooms' 64 px monotone rule over a different
// population and a different ranking, and deliberately a sibling rather than a
// parameterisation (the same call bin/thin-soundings made): the two rules
// disagree on direction AND on the missing-value sentinel, and this one needs
// a coincidence pre-pass the hazard file has no place for.
//
// THE COINCIDENT PAIR
//
// A summit is routinely digitised twice off the same point: an LNDELV spot
// elevation and the SLOGRD ground it stands on, with BIT-IDENTICAL coordinates
// (measured 29-33% of all terrain points in Alaska). Two features, one summit
// -- and two symbols that NEST rather than clash: POSGEN04 is a 9 px dot on
// its pivot, HILTOP01 an empty-centred starburst whose strokes begin ~7 px
// out, so drawn together they read as "sloping ground around the elevation
// point". So exactly-coincident cross-class groups compete as ONE unit per
// copy-ladder interval, and every member shares the unit's outcome:
//
//   * LNDELV represents the group. That is the data talking, not taste: ELEVAT
//     rides LNDELV on 100% of measured rows and SLOGRD on none, and where only
//     one side of a pair is named it is LNDELV 14 times out of 15.
//   * OBJNAM is unioned onto the representative, so a name on the losing side
//     (the 15th case) is kept. When both sides are named and disagree, the
//     LNDELV name wins and the clash is logged.
//   * Only the representative enters the contest, and after the contest every
//     member of its group takes ITS stamped minzoom, so the ground and the
//     spot elevation arrive and leave together. The NAME is still deduped:
//     the _TERRAIN anchor is emitted once, for the representative.
//
// The stated cost: a cell slot won by a coincident group now spends itself on
// two nested icons instead of one, so terrain icon density rises by the
// coincidence rate (29-33% in Alaska, ~3% measured over Puget Sound).
// CELL_PIXELS is the knob if summit clutter regresses.
//
// The group key is the interval, then POSITION -- rounded to 1e-5 degrees,
// about a metre, enough to absorb GeoJSON round-tripping of an identical
// coordinate and nothing more. NEVER the name: "Saddle Mountain" exists twice
// in Alaska 250 km apart, and a name key would merge two real summits.
//
// THE RULE
//
// At each zoom the world is cut into cells of about 64 screen pixels. In each
// cell the highest-priority terrain point wins that zoom, and a feature's
// stamped `minzoom` is the shallowest zoom it ever wins at. Everything else is
// stamped with the band maxzoom, so no summit is ever removed from the chart --
// it arrives later than the better one beside it. Priority, in order:
//
//   1. NAMED first. A named peak is the one a mariner steers by, whatever its
//      height -- and OBJNAM is the only signal SLOGRD carries at all.
//   2. ELEVAT descending. A missing ELEVAT is the LOWEST there is -- the
//      INVERSE of the hazard rule's VALSOU sentinel, for bin/thin-soundings'
//      reason: a missing elevation is not a height claim and cannot displace
//      one, where a missing least depth is the dangerous case and must win.
//   3. Class: LNDELV, then SLOGRD, then SLOTOP -- the surveyed elevation point
//      before the ground it stands on.
//   4. LNAM, so the same chart always produces the same tiles.
//
// The rule is monotone, which is what makes the result stable: a cell at z+1
// is a quarter of a cell at z, so the winner of the larger cell still wins the
// smaller one, and losers stay in the competition at every zoom -- see the
// same paragraph in bin/generate-hazard-minzooms. The competition is per S-57
// CELL, like every generator here, and per copy-ladder interval: a copy
// competes only at the zooms its own interval serves, and what is written is
// the THINNING minzoom alone -- composing it with _QZMIN is
// bin/stamp-quilt-zooms' job (max() of the two floors) and it needs the
// properties to do it.
//
// SLOTOP is accepted for safety, not on evidence: both sampled Alaska
// archives carry ZERO SLOTOP point features (its members are lines). If one
// ever arrives it competes like the rest, ranked after SLOGRD.
//
// _TERRAIN
//
// The generated style has no OBJNAM text for this family, so named peaks are
// unlabelled today. The same pass that decides the icons emits one point per
// surviving NAMED representative -- {OBJNAM, CLASS, ELEVAT, LNAM, INTU} plus
// its interval -- for a Plotroom-owned symbol layer, exactly as _LABELS does
// for the named areas. The anchors carry ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE, not the thinning
// stamp: the named set is thin enough for MapLibre text collision (measured
// max 9 named per 64 px cell at z5), and a label may name a summit whose icon
// arrives later. CSCALE, like every layer's, is stamped at step 3j by
// bin/stamp-quilt-zooms; it is carried here only when the export already had
// one.

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from "fs";

import { ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE } from "./quilt-anchors.mjs";
import { quiltKey, quiltRange } from "./quilt-geojson.mjs";

// Screen size of a thinning cell, in pixels. 64 px is
// bin/generate-hazard-minzooms' spacing, so the terrain symbols thin against
// the same grid as the hazards and the soundings.
const CELL_PIXELS = 64;

// The tile grid the pixel size is measured against. Matches bin/s57-to-tiles.
const TILE_PIXELS = 512;

// Rank at equal name and elevation: the surveyed elevation point before the
// ground it stands on, then the topline. An unknown class ranks last.
const CLASS_RANK = { LNDELV: 0, SLOGRD: 1, SLOTOP: 2 };

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
let minzoom = 0;
let maxzoom = 16;
const inputs = [];
const positional = [];

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === "--minzoom" && args[i + 1]) {
    minzoom = parseInt(args[++i], 10);
  } else if (args[i] === "--maxzoom" && args[i + 1]) {
    maxzoom = parseInt(args[++i], 10);
  } else if (args[i] === "--class" && args[i + 1]) {
    const value = args[++i];
    const separator = value.indexOf(":");
    inputs.push({
      className: value.slice(0, separator),
      path: value.slice(separator + 1),
    });
  } else positional.push(args[i]);
}

const outputPath = positional[0];
if (!outputPath || inputs.length === 0) {
  console.error(
    "Usage: generate-terrain-minzooms <terrain.geojson> --minzoom N " +
      "--maxzoom N --class <CLASS>:<file.geojson> [--class ...]",
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

/** Web Mercator, normalized to [0, 1) on both axes. */
function mercator(lon, lat) {
  const x = (lon + 180) / 360;
  // Clamped short of the poles, where the projection diverges: an S-57 cell
  // never reaches them, and a NaN here would silently unbucket the feature.
  const clamped = Math.max(-85.05112878, Math.min(85.05112878, lat));
  const sin = Math.sin((clamped * Math.PI) / 180);
  const y = 0.5 - Math.log((1 + sin) / (1 - sin)) / (4 * Math.PI);
  return [x, y];
}

/** The feature's name, or null. A blank OBJNAM names nothing. */
function nameOf(properties) {
  const name = properties?.OBJNAM;
  return typeof name === "string" && name.trim() !== "" ? name : null;
}

/**
 * Effective elevation, higher first. Missing ELEVAT is the lowest there is;
 * see THE RULE for why this inverts the hazard sentinel.
 */
function effectiveElevation(properties) {
  const raw = properties?.ELEVAT;
  if (raw === null || raw === undefined || raw === "") {
    return Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  }
  const value = Number(raw);
  return Number.isFinite(value) ? value : Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
}

/**
 * The zoom interval a partitioned copy serves; see THE RULE. An absent or
 * unreadable bound binds nothing, which is every legacy invocation.
 */
function zoomBound(value, fallback) {
  if (value === null || value === undefined || value === "") return fallback;
  const number = Number(value);
  return Number.isFinite(number) ? number : fallback;
}

// Every terrain point across every input, competing as ONE population: a spot
// elevation and the hill under it are two symbols in the same place, and
// thinning each class against itself would leave exactly the pile-up this
// exists to prevent.
const points = [];
const documents = [];

for (const { className, path } of inputs) {
  const document = JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, "utf8"));
  documents.push({ path, document });
  const features = document.features ?? [];
  for (let index = 0; index < features.length; index++) {
    const feature = features[index];
    if (feature?.geometry?.type !== "Point") continue;
    const coordinates = feature.geometry.coordinates;
    if (!Array.isArray(coordinates) || coordinates.length < 2) continue;
    const [lon, lat] = coordinates;
    if (!Number.isFinite(lon) || !Number.isFinite(lat)) continue;
    const properties = feature.properties ?? {};
    const range = quiltRange(properties);
    points.push({
      feature,
      className,
      classRank: CLASS_RANK[className] ?? Object.keys(CLASS_RANK).length,
      name: nameOf(properties),
      elevation: effectiveElevation(properties),
      // The identity a tie is broken on. LNAM is unique across charts; the
      // fallback only has to be stable within this run, and the path plus the
      // export order is.
      key:
        properties.LNAM != null ? String(properties.LNAM) : `${path}#${index}`,
      lon,
      lat,
      mercator: mercator(lon, lat),
      range,
      interval: quiltKey(range),
      qzmin: zoomBound(properties._QZMIN, Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY),
      qzmax: zoomBound(properties._QZMAX, Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY),
      // Filled in below. Nothing that reaches this array leaves without one.
      minzoom: maxzoom,
    });
  }
}

/**
 * The coincidence-group key: interval, then position to 1e-5 degrees. See THE
 * COINCIDENT PAIR -- never the name. Math.round keeps a hair-negative
 * coordinate in the group (String(-0) is "0").
 */
function positionKey(point) {
  const column = Math.round(point.lon * 1e5);
  const row = Math.round(point.lat * 1e5);
  return `${point.interval}\u0000${column},${row}`;
}

/**
 * True when `a` is the better group REPRESENTATIVE: class first (LNDELV
 * carries the elevation and, 14:1, the name), then the contest's own order.
 */
function represents(a, b) {
  if (a.classRank !== b.classRank) return a.classRank < b.classRank;
  if ((a.name !== null) !== (b.name !== null)) return a.name !== null;
  if (a.elevation !== b.elevation) return a.elevation > b.elevation;
  return a.key < b.key;
}

const groups = new Map();
for (const point of points) {
  const id = positionKey(point);
  const group = groups.get(id);
  if (group === undefined) groups.set(id, [point]);
  else group.push(point);
}

let grouped = 0;
const contestants = [];
for (const group of groups.values()) {
  let representative = group[0];
  for (const point of group) {
    if (point !== representative && represents(point, representative)) {
      representative = point;
    }
  }
  representative.group = group;
  for (const point of group) {
    if (point === representative) continue;
    grouped++;
    if (point.name === null) continue;
    if (representative.name === null) {
      // Union OBJNAM: the name rides the representative into the contest and
      // onto its anchor, so a summit named only on the losing side keeps it.
      representative.name = point.name;
      const properties = representative.feature.properties ?? {};
      properties.OBJNAM = point.name;
      representative.feature.properties = properties;
    } else if (representative.name !== point.name) {
      console.error(
        `Coincident ${point.className} "${point.name}" yields to ` +
          `${representative.className} "${representative.name}" at ` +
          `${point.lon},${point.lat}`,
      );
    }
  }
  contestants.push(representative);
}

/** True when `a` outranks `b`; see THE RULE for the order. */
function outranks(a, b) {
  if ((a.name !== null) !== (b.name !== null)) return a.name !== null;
  if (a.elevation !== b.elevation) return a.elevation > b.elevation;
  if (a.classRank !== b.classRank) return a.classRank < b.classRank;
  return a.key < b.key;
}

for (let zoom = minzoom; zoom < maxzoom; zoom++) {
  // Cells per axis at this zoom. 2^zoom tiles of TILE_PIXELS, cut into
  // CELL_PIXELS squares.
  const cells = Math.pow(2, zoom) * (TILE_PIXELS / CELL_PIXELS);
  const winners = new Map();
  for (const point of contestants) {
    if (zoom < point.qzmin || zoom > point.qzmax) continue;
    const [x, y] = point.mercator;
    const column = Math.min(cells - 1, Math.floor(x * cells));
    const row = Math.min(cells - 1, Math.floor(y * cells));
    const cell = `${column},${row}`;
    const held = winners.get(cell);
    if (held === undefined || outranks(point, held)) winners.set(cell, point);
  }
  for (const winner of winners.values()) {
    // Ascending zooms, so the first zoom a feature wins at is the shallowest,
    // and monotonicity means it wins every zoom after it too.
    if (winner.minzoom === maxzoom) winner.minzoom = zoom;
  }
}

// A summit digitised twice is ONE summit: the group competed as one unit and
// every member takes the unit's result, so the sloping ground and the
// elevation point arrive and leave together. The NAME is still deduped -- the
// anchor below is emitted once per representative.
for (const representative of contestants) {
  for (const member of representative.group) {
    member.minzoom = representative.minzoom;
  }
}

for (const point of points) {
  // NOT a property: tippecanoe reads this member off the Feature itself,
  // beside `geometry`. See bin/s57-to-tiles step 4.
  point.feature.tippecanoe = {
    ...(point.feature.tippecanoe ?? {}),
    minzoom: point.minzoom,
  };
}

for (const { path, document } of documents) {
  writeFileSync(path, JSON.stringify(document));
}

const anchors = [];
for (const point of contestants) {
  if (point.name === null) continue;
  const properties = point.feature.properties ?? {};
  anchors.push({
    type: "Feature",
    // See ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE: one anchor per named summit, exempt from
    // dot-dropping and NOT bound to the icon's thinning minzoom.
    tippecanoe: ANCHOR_TIPPECANOE,
    geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: [point.lon, point.lat] },
    properties: {
      OBJNAM: point.name,
      CLASS: point.className,
      ...(properties.ELEVAT == null ? {} : { ELEVAT: properties.ELEVAT }),
      ...(properties.LNAM == null ? {} : { LNAM: properties.LNAM }),
      ...(properties.INTU == null ? {} : { INTU: properties.INTU }),
      // Stamped at step 3j like every layer's; present here only when the
      // export already carried it.
      ...(properties.CSCALE == null ? {} : { CSCALE: properties.CSCALE }),
      // The interval the representative serves, converted to tippecanoe
      // bounds later by bin/stamp-quilt-zooms.
      ...point.range,
    },
  });
}

writeFileSync(
  outputPath,
  JSON.stringify({ type: "FeatureCollection", features: anchors }),
);

const early = points.filter((point) => point.minzoom < maxzoom).length;
console.error(
  `Thinned ${points.length} terrain point(s) across z${minzoom}-${maxzoom}: ` +
    `${early} visible before z${maxzoom}, ${grouped} coincident ` +
    `duplicate(s) grouped, ${anchors.length} named anchor(s)`,
);
